<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The tile the notebook sits on. assets/icon_mk3.svg is drawn on transparency, so this is the
      whole of the icon's ground and the only place its colour exists — mk2 carried a cream #E9DEC7
      rect that stated it, mk3 states nothing. Leaving the background layer transparent is not the
      alternative it sounds like: Android composites both layers regardless, so it would leave the
      notebook floating on the wallpaper rather than producing a tile-less icon.

      WHAT THIS COLOUR DECIDES. Dark and light are not two finishes of one icon here, they are two
      different marks, because the drawing has both a near-white sheet and near-black ink in it and
      the tile picks which of them carries it.

      On the cream this replaces, the mark was a dark object on paper: the cover sat at 4.8:1 and
      the ribbon at 13.9:1, so the ribbon was the first thing the eye found. On #1C1917 that
      inverts. The white middle sheet goes to 17.5:1 and becomes the whole icon — a bright curve of
      pages with a black slit across it — while the ribbon drops to roughly 1:1 against the tile and
      survives only where it crosses the cover and that white edge, read as a gap rather than as a
      stroke. Both work at 48px; this one arguably better, since the brightest element is now the
      one doing the identifying instead of the ground.

      WHY NOT DARKER. #000000 measures better on every element (white sheet 21:1) and was rejected
      anyway: it has no edge of its own against a black wallpaper, so the tile stops existing and the
      icon reads as a floating mark. That is the same failure the old #14100F tile had. #1C1917 is
      lifted just far enough to keep that edge while staying in the app's warm hue family. Going the
      other way, #332C2B washes the cover out to 3.1:1.

      KNOWN COST. mk3's #3F3839 underlays — the two-unit hairline under each dark sheet — sit at
      1.5:1 here and do not register at all. They were drawn to give the dark sheets a defined lower
      edge against a light ground; on this tile the sheets end in the tile instead and the edge that
      separates them is the white sheet above. Do not read their absence as a rendering fault, and do
      not go darkening the tile to try to recover them — that makes it worse. If they ever need to
      show again, the tile is what changes, not the artwork.
    -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#0C0B0B</color>
</resources>
